ere told then by nicholas sturgeon  and the snp that referendum was the gold  standard of referendums  they were abide by the result of the  people of scotland more than 2 million  scots 55 said they wanted to remain part  of the united kingdom  more than 1.6 million wanted to remain  in the eu  too now i think most polls would tell us  the majority  position in scotland and of course the  other point here is that things  change pretty dramatically after 2014  the brexit referendum  whereby scotland has been taken out of  the eu against our will  really changed fundamentally the nature  of the  uk the conservatives were the difficult  position to defend  more than a million people in scotland  did vote to leave the european union and  when we voted in 2016 we knew it was  part of a uk  2 of scottish people wanted to remain  in the european union do you not think  that nicola sturgeon and the s  p have an argument to say that scottish  people  are not being represented we knew in  2016 the votes taken in scotland england  wales and northern ireland would be  combined together for a uk result  whether to leave  or stay in the eu brexit has given a  second independence referendum impetus  while an snp majority doesn't guarantee  independence  it does guarantee a fight if we win the  elections then we will bring forward the  legislation  and if boris johnson wants to challenge  that  legally which is what he would have to  do then we will rigorously and  vigorously defend the position  against that challenge no prime minister  is  immune to democracy even boris johnson  if it does happen  how does the prime minister of the  united kingdom  try and stop as second reflectors do you  just say that we're not doing it  well you're going to be really hard well  i don't think you would  expect me to to concede an election  before the votes have been  cast before they've been counted and  before we've seen an opportunity again  as we did in 2016  for the scottish conservatives 